WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:10.470
In this part of mesh optimizations, I want to talk about level of details, as these are very important for both your static and your skeletal meshes.

2
00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:15.330
Now before I jump into what they are, I'd like to make one note about Nanite.

3
00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:21.829
Nanite is going to do a lot of this work for you automatically, so if you're working on a project that utilizes Nanite meshes,

4
00:00:21.829 --> 00:00:30.089
you can still adjust the level of details, but you do not need to do as much work as you do with just standard static or skeletal meshes.

5
00:00:30.089 --> 00:00:36.850
Now, with that out of the way, what is a level of detail? Well, on the screen in front of us, we have this little cow taken from the content examples,

6
00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:46.049
and we have it broken up into four different meshes. Now at the top of this image above each mesh is the number of triangles,

7
00:00:46.049 --> 00:00:51.890
and at the bottom is the level of detail, or the LOD level that is associated with it.

8
00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:57.689
And what level of details do is they give us the ability to show the exact same mesh,

9
00:00:57.950 --> 00:01:02.950
but as that mesh gets further and further away from the camera, the number of triangles is reduced,

10
00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:11.950
and just as long as we keep that silhouette intact and reduce those triangles, to the user, this is going to be an almost unnoticeable difference.

11
00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:20.030
Now, level of detail meshes can be created for both static and skeletal meshes using a similar interface.

12
00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:25.950
How this works is we define the level or number of level of details that we wish,

13
00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:30.950
and then for each level, we can adjust the criteria for reduction.

14
00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:38.450
We will have the ability to choose between triangles and vertices, and then a percentage that we want to keep for that particular level.

15
00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:42.450
Let's take a look at this inside of the editor with this static mesh.

16
00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:49.299
Now, right now, this static mesh has three levels of detail applied to it, so that's one, that's two,

17
00:01:49.299 --> 00:01:54.799
and then if I go all the way back out here, we've got to go kind of far, but that is our third level of detail.

18
00:01:54.799 --> 00:02:02.299
So you can see here, again, if we were this close right here with the default, let's take a look at this actually by grabbing it and hitting CTRL-E,

19
00:02:02.299 --> 00:02:04.799
and then I'm going to bring this on the screen for us.

20
00:02:04.799 --> 00:02:13.800
So we can see here, by default, this thing's 57,000 triangles, so when we are this far away, it just doesn't make sense to draw that many triangles.

21
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:21.300
We can barely see it, we just kind of see the outline of it, so that's why we want to reduce that when it's in the distance.

22
00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:28.800
So to do this, what we're going to do is we're going to come up here to our LOD picker, and we can come down and select the individual LOD levels,

23
00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:38.800
and I got this LOD picker by first coming here to our LOD settings, and number of LODs we can increase or decrease this number to give us more or less LODs.

24
00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:52.300
And once we set that, let's go ahead and come over here to LOD, let's say LOD 3, and look at this, this is at 1146, and it does look a little decimally,

25
00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:55.800
so let's set the triangle amount back to say 5 and hit apply changes.

26
00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:03.300
We are going to need to give this a second to process because it's got to go and redo all of the level of detail levels that it has, recalculate everything.

27
00:03:05.439 --> 00:03:12.439
Alright, so that is a little bit better, look at that, it's only 2800, so it's about 500 more than it was before, so that's looking pretty good.

28
00:03:12.439 --> 00:03:18.439
Let's take a look at this in our project now, so we're going to close this, and then we just need to kind of zoom out a little bit more,

29
00:03:18.439 --> 00:03:23.439
and there we go, that is looking a little bit better, and I think that's a pretty good result right there,

30
00:03:23.439 --> 00:03:28.439
I mean 2000 for something that's going to be that far away is not too bad, so as we zoom in on that,

31
00:03:28.439 --> 00:03:35.439
you can see here the level of detail is going to automatically change for us as the camera moves around the scene.

32
00:03:35.439 --> 00:03:42.030
Now, skeletal meshes can also have this applied, but when working with skeletal meshes,

33
00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:52.060
you're going to find a few additional controls and settings, as sometimes the reduction might happen in locations that are not ideal.

34
00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:58.560
In the example in the screen on front of us, we see the mannequin hand, and with the default reduction,

35
00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:04.560
notice that the finger geometry is kind of collapsing, and those don't really look too much like fingers anymore.

36
00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:09.560
On the right hand side though, we've enabled an option called bone priority reduction,

37
00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:18.560
and what this does is, this says, hey, when you're going to reduce, we're going to say remove geometry everywhere except for the hands,

38
00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:24.060
and that's right, we can give the bones that the geometry and the hands are skinned to a priority,

39
00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:30.060
and things with a higher priority will be very last to have their geometry removed.

40
00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:37.129
Now, the next setting that I want to talk about happens to deal with open mesh edges.

41
00:04:37.129 --> 00:04:43.129
On our skeletal meshes, we can also lock our mesh edges, and the reason I'm bringing this up is

42
00:04:43.129 --> 00:04:47.629
because of the highlighted or circled area in the image on the screen in front of us.

43
00:04:47.629 --> 00:04:54.129
Oftentimes when you're dealing with games, particularly games that have customization,

44
00:04:54.129 --> 00:05:01.129
you generally separate the head from the torso, the torso from the legs, and possibly the arms depending on your setup.

45
00:05:01.129 --> 00:05:07.129
Because of this, it will introduce what are known as open edges, and a lot of times there'll be geometry

46
00:05:07.129 --> 00:05:14.129
that needs to fit inside of this open edge to complete the edge loop to make it look like it's one complete unit.

47
00:05:14.129 --> 00:05:22.660
If you do not run the logarithm with lock mesh edges on, it will remove vertices, like in this example.

48
00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:29.660
We see in the highlighted area there's a vert that needs to stay there in order for the head to meet with the torso.

49
00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:35.660
And again, our tools are very powerful. They have the option to run the logarithm with this on,

50
00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:42.660
which will exclude any open edges from the reduction, ensuring that your heads and your torsos and your legs and your feet

51
00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:49.160
will all be put together and you won't see any gaps or holes inside of the model.

52
00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:57.319
One last thing to note about level of detail adjustments, while Nanite does do this for you,

53
00:05:57.319 --> 00:06:03.819
you will need to set your level of detail adjustments on any and all skeletal meshes that you have.

54
00:06:03.819 --> 00:06:07.319
Let's take a look at how we do that inside of the editor now.

55
00:06:07.319 --> 00:06:13.819
So I'm going to go ahead and hit control space here, and we're going to come here to our third person BP,

56
00:06:13.819 --> 00:06:22.269
to our mannequin, character, mesh, and we're going to open up our skeletal mesh right here.

57
00:06:22.269 --> 00:06:30.769
So here is our skeletal mesh, and to get to our LOD, we have our LOD settings right here.

58
00:06:30.769 --> 00:06:35.269
And just like with our static mesh, we have the number of LODs that we can set.

59
00:06:35.269 --> 00:06:39.769
However, we do have some different settings inside of our reduction.

60
00:06:39.769 --> 00:06:46.769
We have triangles, vertices, first percent satisfied, max triangles, max vertices, and max first satisfied.

61
00:06:46.769 --> 00:06:55.769
So, triangles is how many triangles do we want this to remove, vertices is the amount of vertices that we want this to move,

62
00:06:55.769 --> 00:06:59.769
and first percent satisfied allows us to pick between the two.

63
00:06:59.769 --> 00:07:09.769
So we can set, I want you to remove up to 12,000 vertices, or 12,000 triangles, and whichever one of those it can reduce first, it will stay there.

64
00:07:09.769 --> 00:07:15.769
This next one, max triangles and max vertices, and first max satisfied, these are pretty cool.

65
00:07:15.769 --> 00:07:18.269
This allows us to actually set a maximum number.

66
00:07:18.269 --> 00:07:21.769
Let's say we want to reduce this down to 1,000.

67
00:07:21.769 --> 00:07:27.769
We can put this in here, the logarithm will go, and it will reduce it right down to that number,

68
00:07:27.769 --> 00:07:30.769
or a little bit above, or a tiny bit below.

69
00:07:30.769 --> 00:07:35.769
And we can do that again with our max triangles, or our max vertices.

70
00:07:35.769 --> 00:07:43.959
A few other options that I would like to point out here are things like our volumetric correction.

71
00:07:43.959 --> 00:07:50.459
Here is our lock mesh edge option, as well as enforce bone boundaries.

72
00:07:50.459 --> 00:07:57.459
And if we come up to our LOD info, our bones to prioritize is where we can add a list of bones,

73
00:07:57.459 --> 00:08:02.459
and then we need to make sure that we set the weight of prioritization to a very high number,

74
00:08:02.459 --> 00:08:09.459
so that when we run our reduction, it will only take things from that particular area.

75
00:08:09.459 --> 00:08:12.459
Let's go ahead and take a look at our LOD right now.

76
00:08:12.459 --> 00:08:18.529
So here's our LOD 3, and we can see here we've got some mangled fingers right there.

77
00:08:18.529 --> 00:08:26.529
So what we're going to do is come to our character, we're going to go to our bones and show our bone names, and that's index 03L.

78
00:08:26.529 --> 00:08:31.689
So what I'm going to do is bones to prioritize, I'm going to add a new item to the list here.

79
00:08:31.689 --> 00:08:39.629
I'm going to scroll down, and we're going to look for, it was index 03L.

80
00:08:39.629 --> 00:08:43.629
And now what I'm going to do with that, I'm going to say regenerate LOD.

81
00:08:43.629 --> 00:08:49.200
And it looks like I might have not gotten that right, so one more time here.

82
00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:56.389
Let's go ahead and show bones, bone names, index 03.

83
00:08:56.389 --> 00:09:10.909
Let's go back up here real quick.

84
00:09:10.909 --> 00:09:23.539
Maybe we actually need to do the one above it, so let's just add another one here.

85
00:09:23.539 --> 00:09:25.539
And let's regenerate.

86
00:09:25.539 --> 00:09:29.100
Hey, there we go, alright.

87
00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:34.100
So it's actually index 02 that was the culprit, but you see how easy that was?

88
00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:41.100
One of the other things that is really cool about this too, is let's just turn off our bone names here.

89
00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:48.490
Let's come back up to our reduction settings here.

90
00:09:48.490 --> 00:09:52.490
And we'll increase this to say 10.

91
00:09:52.490 --> 00:09:55.490
Alright, and then we're going to say regenerate LOD.

92
00:09:55.490 --> 00:10:02.289
Now, there we go, that put in a little bit more geometry in there for us.

93
00:10:02.289 --> 00:10:10.289
Now, one of the things that you often have to do when you make your LODs is you need to make sure that they work with the animations as well.

94
00:10:10.289 --> 00:10:13.289
So let's come over here to our animation tab.

95
00:10:13.289 --> 00:10:16.289
And look at our third person.

96
00:10:16.289 --> 00:10:20.500
Let's see if we got maybe the idle here or something like that.

97
00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:21.500
There we go.

98
00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:22.500
So here is our idle.

99
00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:25.500
And let's just bring this down to our third LOD.

100
00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:27.500
And what I'm going to do is I'm going to come back here.

101
00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:30.500
And this I think is one of the coolest things about this tool.

102
00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:35.500
So I'm going to take this volumetric correction and set it to a value of 1 and hit regenerate.

103
00:10:35.500 --> 00:10:40.399
Now, normally if I wanted to fix this, now it gives them a super pointy head.

104
00:10:40.399 --> 00:10:44.399
What I would have to do is make this correction inside my DCC.

105
00:10:44.399 --> 00:10:51.399
Make sure that the skinned weights are transferred over and that basically nothing is out of place.

106
00:10:51.399 --> 00:10:56.399
And that's just a lot of time, even if you have tools that are set up to automatically do that for you.

107
00:10:56.399 --> 00:10:57.399
Now check this out.

108
00:10:57.399 --> 00:11:00.399
Watch what happens when I come back over here to my animated.

109
00:11:00.399 --> 00:11:02.529
Notice it looks exactly the same.

110
00:11:02.529 --> 00:11:06.529
So let's just fix that so his head is not so pointy.

111
00:11:06.529 --> 00:11:09.830
And we'll give this a second.

112
00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:10.830
So there we go.

113
00:11:10.830 --> 00:11:12.830
Now, no longer a super pointy head.

114
00:11:12.830 --> 00:11:13.830
Let's come back to our animation.

115
00:11:13.830 --> 00:11:15.830
Look at that, no longer super pointy head.

116
00:11:15.830 --> 00:11:22.220
So this is really, really awesome because this means that we can adjust all of our LODs.

117
00:11:22.220 --> 00:11:24.220
We can quickly make changes.

118
00:11:24.220 --> 00:11:25.220
We can remove them.

119
00:11:25.220 --> 00:11:26.220
We can add them.

120
00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:29.220
And we can do it all in a non-destructive manner.

121
00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:35.220
And that's what makes these tools so powerful is that it's the non-destructive nature of them.

122
00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:42.309
Allows us to quickly iterate, run tests and optimizations that would otherwise take too long

123
00:11:42.309 --> 00:11:49.309
or require too many different people in order to see if the actual optimization is a performance

124
00:11:49.309 --> 00:11:51.309
gain or a performance decrement.
